---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="AI Test Plan - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="testing" subChapterId="ai-test-plan">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				One of the fastest ways to improve your testing is to have the AI review your code and generate a list of scenarios to test. This works whether you
				are writing automated tests or building a manual test plan for QA. The AI reads the code, identifies branches and edge cases, and produces a
				structured list you can work from.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
				<p>
					Even if you feel confident about your implementation, running an AI-generated test plan will often surface edge cases you did not consider during
					development. Boundary conditions, unusual input combinations, and race conditions are the kinds of things that are easy to overlook when you are
					focused on building the feature.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>Generating a Test Plan from Code</h3>

			<p>
				Point the AI at a branch or a specific set of changed files and ask it to produce test scenarios. The AI will trace through the logic and identify
				cases you might miss, especially around error handling, boundary conditions, and interactions between components.
			</p>

			<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
				<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Example prompt</div>
				<p class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
					Review the changes on this branch and generate a test plan. For each scenario, include what to test, the expected result, and any setup steps
					needed. Group them by feature area.
				</p>
			</div>

			<p>
				The output will typically cover the happy path, validation failures, edge cases around empty or null inputs, and permission checks. It will not
				always catch business logic nuances that depend on context the AI does not have, so treat the list as a starting point rather than a finished plan.
			</p>

			<h3>Test It Yourself First</h3>

			<p>
				Before handing a feature to QA, run through the AI-generated test plan yourself. This catches the obvious issues early and saves your QA team from
				reporting bugs you could have found in two minutes. It also helps you validate that the test plan itself makes sense. If a scenario is unclear or
				impossible to reproduce, fix the plan before it reaches someone else.
			</p>

			<p>
				Faster turnaround on finding bugs saves everyone time. 
			</p>

			<h3>Iterating on the Plan</h3>

			<p>
				If the AI's first pass misses areas you care about, tell it. "You missed testing for concurrent users editing the same record" or "Add scenarios for
				when the external API returns a 500" will get you a more complete plan. The AI responds well to specific gaps because it can immediately trace through
				and find the "expected" result.
			</p>

			<p>
				You can also feed it your existing test suite and ask what is missing. "Here are our current tests for the payment module. What scenarios are we not
				covering?" gives you a gap analysis without having to audit every file yourself.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
				<p>
					If you are using an agent-based tool like Claude Code, you can ask it to run the tests after generating them. Let it see the failures directly
					and iterate until they pass.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>Automated vs. Manual Test Plans</h3>

			<p>
				The AI can generate both. For automated tests, it will write the actual test code. For manual test plans, it will produce a structured document with
				steps, expected results, and preconditions. Specify which format you need upfront so the output is immediately useful.
			</p>

			<p>
				For automated tests, give the AI an existing test file as a reference so it matches your project's patterns, assertion library, and naming
				conventions. Without that reference, you will spend time reformatting the output to fit your codebase.
			</p>

			<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
				<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Example prompt</div>
				<p class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
					Write unit tests for the CartService class. Use the existing tests in src/tests/order-service.test.ts as a reference for style and structure.
					Cover the add, remove, and checkout methods, including edge cases for empty carts and invalid item IDs. Try to be concise with your tests and don't add duplicate tests.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
